\id JOS - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.19
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Joshua
\toc1 Joshua
\toc2 Joshua
\mt1 Joshua
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Yahweh encourages Joshua
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 After the death of Yahweh’s servant Moses, Yahweh spoke to Moses’s servant Joshua, Nun’s son), saying,
\v 2 “Now that my servant Moses is dead, you and all these people should pack up \add and\add* cross over this Jordan \add river\add* \add to go\add* to the land that I am about to give to them—to Israel’s descendants.
\v 3 I’ll give you all every place that the soles of your feet tread on, just like I told Moses.
\v 4 Your territory will extend from the wilderness \add in the south\add* to the Lebanon \add mountains in the north\add*, and from the large Euphrates river \add in the east\add*, and right to the Mediterranean sea in the west, including all of the Hittites’ land.
\v 5 No man will be able to challenge your leadership as long as you live—I’ll be with you just as I was with Moses—I won’t fail you or abandon you.
\v 6 Be strong and be courageous, for you yourself will lead these people to inherit the land that I promised their ancestors that I’d give to them.
\v 7 Just be strong and very courageous—making sure that you obey everything that my servant Moses instructed you. Don’t turn away to the right or to the left from it, so that you’ll be wise in every situation that you end up in.
\v 8 That book of the law must always be on your lips and you should meditate on it day and night, so that you’ll be careful to follow everything that’s written in it, because then you’ll make your ways prosperous and then you’ll be wise.
\v 9 Didn’t I order you to be strong and courageous? Don’t tremble or be discouraged because your god Yahweh will be with you wherever you go.”
\s1 Joshua instructs the people
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 10 Then Joshua instructed the leaders of the people,
\v 11 “Go right through the camp and tell the people: Get everything ready that you’ll all need because in three days we’ll be ready to cross over this river and enter the land that your god Yahweh is about to give to you, and to take control of it.”
\p
\v 12 To the descendants of Reuben and Gad, and to the Manasseh half-tribe, Joshua said,
\v 13 “Remember what Yahweh’s servant Moses informed you: Your god Yahweh is about give this land to you and to enable you all to settle down.
\v 14 Your women and children and your livestock will stay \add here\add* in the land that Moses allocated to you on this eastern side of the Jordan \add river\add*. But your \add soldiers\add* must dress for battle and cross over ahead of your brothers and help them
\v 15 until Yahweh enables your brothers to take over the land that your god Yahweh is about to give them so they can settle like you all have. Then you’ll all be able to return to this land which Yahweh’s servant Moses gave you on this eastern side of the Jordan and take full control of it.”
\p
\v 16 “We’ll do everything you’ve told us to,” the people answered, “and we’ll go wherever you send us.
\v 17 We’ll listen to you just like we listened to everything Moses said. May your god Yahweh be with you, just like he was with Moses.
\v 18 We’ll put anyone to death who rebels against your words or refuses to follow your instructions. Just stay strong and courageous.”
\c 2
\s1 Spies sent to Jericho
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then from \add there at\add* Acacia Grove, Joshua Nun’s son) secretly sent off two spies, saying, “Go and learn about the land over there, especially around Jericho \add city\add*.” So they went off, and \add over there,\add* they entered the house of a woman—a prostitute named Rahab—and they lay down there.
\v 2 However, someone told Jericho’s king, “Listen, some Israelite men arrived here tonight to check out our land.”
\v 3 So the king sent \add guards\add* to tell Rahab, “Bring out the men who went to your place and stayed the night, because they’ve come here to check out our land!”
\p
\v 4 However, Rahab had hidden the two men so she answered, “Indeed, the men arrived here, but I didn’t know where they were from.
\v 5 But as the \add city\add* gate was about to shut last night, the men went out. I don’t know to where they went—if you all hurry, you might be able to catch them.”
\v 6 (But actually she had taken them up onto her \add flat\add* roof and hidden them in bundles of flax that were drying there.)
\v 7 So the guards hurried out on the road to the fords on the Jordan \add river\add* and the \add city\add* gate was kept shut after they had left.
\p
\v 8 Before the Israelite men lay down \add to sleep that night\add*, Rahab went up them on the roof
\v 9 and told them, “We know that Yahweh has given this land to you all and we’re all terrified of you. Everyone that lives here is trembling at the thought of your coming
\v 10 because we’ve heard that Yahweh dried up the water of the Red sea ahead of you all when you left Egypt. And we heard what you all did to two of the Amorite kings Sihon and Og over the Jordan there—how you all completely destroyed them.
\v 11 Yes, we’ve heard the reports and we trembled and lost our courage to fight you all because your god Yahweh, he’s God over the heavens above and the earth below.
\v 12 So now, please promise me by Yahweh that because I’ve been kind to \add both of\add* you, you all will act kindly towards my father’s household. Give me a guarantee that you’ll do what you promise
\v 13 and spare my parents and my siblings and their families from death.”
\p
\v 14 “Our lives for your lives,” the \add two\add* men agreed. “If you don’t tell anyone about our agreement, then when Yahweh gives us this land, we’ll show kindness and faithfulness to you.”
\p
\v 15 Then she put a rope out through the window to let them down \add to escape from the city\add*, because her house where she lived was \add actually built into\add* the side of the wall.
\v 16 “Go to the hill country,” she told them, “so those searching for you won’t find you, and hide \add up\add* there for three days. \add Then once\add* the searchers have returned, you’ll be able to \add safely\add* return to your camp.”
\p
\v 17 “This promise that you made us make,” the men told her, “won’t apply unless you do this:
\v 18 \add Take\add* this red cord \add and\add* when we invade the land, tie it in this window in the wall. Make sure that your parents and your siblings and their households are all here in your house.
\v 19 If any of them leaves the house, their life will then be in their own hands and we’ll be innocent \add if they’re killed\add*. But if anyone with you in this house gets injured, we’ll be responsible for that.
\v 20 Also, if you tell anyone else about this agreement, then we’ll be released from this promise that you made us make.”
\v 21 “Sure, let it be as you say,” Rahab responded. Then she sent them off, and after they’d gone, she tied the red cord in her window.
\p
\v 22 The two men went up into the hills and stayed there for three days until the search party returned \add to the city\add* after searching all the roads and not finding them.
\v 23 So the two men went down \add to the river\add* and crossed over and returned to Joshua Nun’s son), and they told him everything that had happened.
\v 24 “Yahweh has certainly given this land to us,” they told Joshua. “The people there won’t be able to resist us because they’re so terrified.”
\c 3
\s1 The Israelites cross the Jordan
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Early the next morning, Joshua \add and the people\add* got up early and set off from Acacia Grove and went as far as the Jordan \add river\add* and camped there without crossing over.
\v 2 After three days, the leaders went throughout the camp
\v 3 and instructed the people, “As soon as you all see the box \add containing\add* the agreement with your god Yahweh, and see the Levitical priests carrying it, then you yourselves should move out from your places and follow after it.
\v 4 But keep about nine hundred metres back from it. Don’t go any closer than that so that you all can see the way to go, because we haven’t been this way before.”
\p
\v 5 Then Joshua told the people, “Make sure you all do the ritual washing to make yourselves pure, because tomorrow Yahweh will do miracles among us.”
\v 6 And he told the priests, “Lift up the box with the agreement, and walk across here in front of the people.” So they lifted up the \add sacred\add* chest and went and stood in front of all the people.
\p
\v 7 Then Yahweh told Joshua, “Today I’ll begin to make you powerful in the eyes of all Israel so they’ll know that I’m with you just like I was with Moses.
\v 8 Tell the priests who are carrying the \add sacred\add* chest that as soon as they come to the edge of the river, they should go in just a little and then stop there.”
\p
\v 9 Then Joshua told the Israelites, “Gather around here and listen to your god Yahweh’s message.”
\v 10 He told them, “This is how you’ll all know that God, who can do anything, is among you. With your own eyes, you’ll all see how he’ll take away the land from the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Hivim, the Perizim, the Girgashim, the Amorites, and the Jebusites.
\v 11 Listen, the \add sacred\add* chest containing the slabs that belongs to the master who \add owns\add* the whole world, is about to \add be taken\add* across the Jordan in front of you.
\v 12 So now, select one man from each of your twelve tribes,
\v 13 and as soon as the priests carrying the sacred chest go into the Jordan, as soon as their feet touch the water, the river water on the upstream side will pile up in a heap.”
\p
\v 14 So once the people had packed up their tents ready to cross the river, the priests carrying the \add sacred\add* box walked ahead in front of them.
\v 15 Then as soon as those priests steeped into the \add edge\add* of the fast-flowing river (because it overflowed its banks at harvest time \add in the spring\add*),
\v 16 then the water coming from upstream \add suddenly\add* stopped flowing. The water far upstream piled up near a town called Adam (near Tseretan), and the water that normally flowed down towards the Aravah sea (\add also called\add* the Dead sea) stopped flowing, so the people were able to cross \add what was\add* the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho.
\v 17 The priests carrying the \add sacred\add* chest with Yahweh’s agreement \add in it\add*, stood on firm, dry ground in \add what had been\add* the middle of the river. All the Israelites crossed over on dry ground until everyone was across.
\c 4
\s1 The memorial of boulders
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Once all the people had crossed over the Jordan \add river\add*, Yahweh told Joshua,
\v 2 “Take \add those\add* twelve men that the people selected from each tribe,
\v 3 and give them these instructions: Pick up twelve boulders from here in the middle of the Jordan from near where the priests are standing, and bring them over with you, and put them in the place where you all camp tonight.”
\p
\v 4 So Joshua called \add over\add* the twelve men, one from each tribe,
\v 5 and told them, “Cross back to where your god Yahweh’s box is in the middle of the Jordan and each of you should pick up a rock and carry it back on your shoulder—one stone each on your shoulder to match the number of tribes of Israel’s sons.
\v 6 Those boulders will be a monument for all the people. In the future when your children ask what those boulders mean,
\v 7 then you’ll all be able to tell them that the water in the Jordan \add river\add* was cut off ahead of the box with Yahweh’s agreement. When it crossed over into the river, the water in the river stopped flowing, and those boulders are a memorial for the Israelites forever.
\p
\v 8 So the Israelites did just what Joshua had told them to do, and they picked up twelve boulders from the Jordan, just as Yahweh had instructed Joshua, and brought them over to where \add the people\add* were camping and placed them down there.
\v 9 Then Joshua stacked up twelve other boulders in the middle of the Jordan where the feet of the priests carrying the box with the agreement had been, and they remain there until today.
\v 10 The priests carrying the box had stood in the middle of the Jordan until everything that Yahweh had instructed Joshua to pass on to the people was done, just as Moses had \add also\add* instructed Joshua, and the people had rapidly crossed over.
\p
\v 11 As soon as all the people had crossed over, the priests carrying Yahweh’s box \add also\add* crossed over in front of the people.
\v 12 The \add soldiers\add* from the Reuben and Gad tribes and the Manasseh half-tribe had marched over ahead of the rest of the Israelite, as Moses had agreed with them.
\v 13 (\add There were\add* around forty thousand of them, armed and ready for battle, and they were heading to the Jericho plains for the battle \add there\add*.)
\v 14 On that day, Yahweh helped Joshua to look powerful in the eyes of the people, and they respected him just as they had respected Moses all the days of his\f + \fr 4:14 \ft The Hebrew doesn’t make it clear here whether the writer meant all of \+bd Moses’s life\+bd* or all of \+bd Joshua’s life\+bd*, so translations will vary.\f* life.
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh told Joshua,
\v 16 “Now order the priests carrying the box with the agreement to come up out of \add what was\add* the Jordan \add river\add*,”
\v 17 so Joshua instructed the priests to come on up from the Jordan.
\v 18 As soon as the priests carrying the box with Yahweh’s agreement came up out of \add what had been\add* the Jordan \add river\add* and their feet stepped onto \add what had been\add* dry land, the river water returned to its place and overflowed its banks as it had been doing for the previous few days.
\p
\v 19 The people had come up from the river valley around the end of March and they camped east of Jericho at Gilgal,
\v 20 which was where they had set up the \add monument of\add* twelve boulders.
\v 21 Then Joshua told the Israelites, “In the future when your descendants ask why these boulders are here,
\v 22 tell them that this is where Israel crossed the Jordan \add river\add* on dry land.
\v 23 Your god Yahweh dried up the river water ahead of you until you’d all crossed over, just as he had done when he dried up the Red sea before we crossed over.
\v 24 Yahweh did that so that all the peoples in the world would know that he is powerful, \add and\add* so that you’ll always all respect him.
\c 5
\p
\v 1 When all the Amorite kings who were on the \add west\add* side of the Jordan (towards the ocean) and all the Canaanite kings (beside the ocean) heard how Yahweh had dried up the water in the Jordan \add river\add* until the Israelites had crossed over, they trembled with fear and lost their nerve to stand against Israel.
\s1 Circumcising the males and celebrating ‘pass-over’ at Gilgal
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 2 At that time, Yahweh told Joshua, “Make yourself flint knives and again circumcise the male Israelites.”
\v 3 So Joshua \add had\add* flint knives made and the \add male\add* Israelites were circumcised at \add a place they named\add* ‘The hill of foreskins’.
\v 4 (That was done because all the men who’d left Egypt and who were old enough to join the army \add had been circumcised but\add* had died in the wilderness since then,
\v 5 \add and\add* because all the boys who’d been born while they were in the wilderness after they’d left Egypt hadn’t been circumcised.
\v 6 The Israelites had spent forty years moving around the wilderness until the last of those men who’d left Egypt but hadn’t listened to Yahweh had died. Yahweh had promised not to let them see the land that he’d promised to their ancestors to give them—a land that’s very fertile.
\v 7 It was the sons of those people that Joshua had circumcised \add this time\add* because they hadn’t been circumcised on the way.)
\p
\v 8 After all their males had been circumcised, they remained in that camp until they’d healed up.
\v 9 Then Yahweh told Joshua, “Today I’ve rolled away the disgrace of Egypt from you,” and that place is named Gilgal\f + \fr 5:9 \ft The name ‘Gilgal’ (‘גִּלְגָּל’, ‘circle’) sounds like ‘galal’ (‘גַּלַל’, ‘roll’).\f* until this day.
\p
\v 10 In the evening of the fourth day that the people had been camped at Gilgal there on the plains near Jericho, they celebrated the ‘pass-over’.
\s1 Eating from the land—no more manna
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 11 The next day they ate roasted grain that \add they’d taken from that area\add*, and ate flat bread that same day.
\v 12 The day after they’d eaten some of the produce from that area, there was no manna \add on the ground to be collected\add* and \add so\add* it stopped—instead they’d eat the produce from the Canaan region \add beginning\add* that year.
\s1 Joshua and the man with a sword
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 13 While Joshua was in the Jericho \add area\add*, he looked up and saw a man in front of him. The man had drawn his sword and was holding it in his hand so Joshua approached him and asked, “Are you on \em our\em* side, or with our enemies?”
\p
\v 14 “No,” the man answered, “but I, the leader of Yahweh’s army, have come now.”
\p Then Joshua fell to his knees and bowed down with his face to the ground, and asked him, “What’s my master going to command to his servant?”
\p
\v 15 “Take off your sandals,” the commander of Yahweh’s army told him, “because the ground that you’re standing is holy.” So Joshua took them off.
\c 6
\s1 Jericho’s walls collapse
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Now Jericho \add city\add* was shut-up tightly because of the Israelites, \add so\add* no one was coming out and no one could go in.
\v 2 Then Yahweh told Joshua, “Listen, I’ve given you victory over Jericho and its king, and their mighty army warriors.
\v 3 All your soldiers must go around the city once every day for six days
\v 4 with seven priests blowing ram’s horns going ahead of the \add sacred\add* box. On the seventh day, you all must go around the city seven times while the priests are blowing the horns.
\v 5 Finally, with a long blast of the horn, when you all hear that then all the people must shout loudly and the wall around the city will fall down flat, and all the soldiers should go straight in \add from where they’re standing\add*.”
\p
\v 6 So Joshua summoned the priests and told them, “Carry the box with the agreement, and have seven priests hold up seven rams’ horns ahead of Yahweh’s box.”
\v 7 Then he told the people, “Go across in front and go around the city, with the soldiers going ahead of Yahweh’s box.”
\p
\v 8 After Joshua had spoken to the people, the seven priests with their seven rams’ horns went across in front and blew the horns, and the box with Yahweh’s covenant was carried along behind them.
\v 9 The soldiers walked ahead of the priests blowing the horns, with the rear guard walking behind the box. \add So\add* they were walking and blowing the horns,
\v 10 and Joshua told the people, “Don’t shout or even speak or make a noise until the day I tell you all to shout—then you all should shout loudly.”
\p
\v 11 So Yahweh’s box was carried once around the city, then the people went back to the camp and stayed the night there.
\p
\v 12 Joshua got up early the next morning, and the priests picked up Yahweh’s box
\v 13 and the seven priests holding the seven rams’ horns went in front of it—blowing the horns as they kept walking. The soldiers walking in front of them, and the rear guard walked behind Yahweh’s box while the horns were being blown.
\v 14 They went once around the city on that second day and then returned to their camp \add again\add*—doing that for six days.
\p
\v 15 Then on the seventh day they got up early at dawn and went around the city as before, except this day they went around seven times.
\v 16 After that seventh time around, the priests blew \add a long blast\add* on the rams’ horns and Joshua told the people, “Shout, because Yahweh has given you all the city!
\v 17 \add However,\add* everything in the city is sacred to Yahweh. Only the prostitute Rahab and those who are in her house will be allowed to live, because she hid the spies that we sent in.
\v 18 But for all of you, everything inside the city is forbidden—if any of you take anything out of the city then our camp would also then be marked for destruction and it would cause \add a lot of\add* trouble.
\v 19 All the silver and gold, bronze and iron is dedicated to Yahweh and must go into his treasury.
\p
\v 20 So the people shouted when that horn blew—as soon as they heard it the people shouted loudly and the wall fell down on itself and the people went up \add and over\add* into the city from wherever they were standing and captured the city.
\v 21 They killed every \add living thing\add* in the city with their swords, including men and women, young and old, even cattle, sheep, and donkeys.
\p
\v 22 Then Joshua commanded the two men who had spied on the area, “Go to the prostitute’s house and bring the woman out of it and all who are related to her, just as you promised her.”
\v 23 So the young men who’d gone in as spies went in and brought out Rahab and her parents and all her relatives and settled them outside of the camp of the Israelites.
\v 24 Then the warriors set fire to the city and burnt everything that was in it. Only the silver and gold, and bronze and iron were \add first removed and\add* put into the treasury of Yahweh’s house.
\v 25 But Joshua spared the prostitute Rahab and her father’s household and her other relatives because she hid the men that Joshua had sent in to spy on Jericho, and she still lives in Israel to this day.
\p
\v 26 At that time, Joshua made the people take an oath, saying,
\q1 “May Yahweh curse anyone who rebuilds this city, Jericho.
\q1 When that person lays its foundation, may his oldest son die.
\q1 And when he \add finishes building the city wall and\add* sets up its gates, may his youngest son die.”
\p
\v 27 So Yahweh was with Joshua and everyone in that region had heard about him.
\c 7
\s1 Akan’s sin
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 However the Israelites disobeyed the command about not taking anything. Akan (son of Karmi, son of Zavdi, son of Zerah, from the Judah tribe) took something that was prohibited, so Yahweh was angry with the Israelites.
\p
\v 2 Meanwhile, Joshua sent some of his men from Jericho to Ai (east of Bethel and near Beyt-Aven) and told them, “Go to Ai and spy out the land.” So the men went and checkout out Ai.
\v 3 When they returned to Joshua, they told him, “Don’t send all the warriors—\add only\add* around two to three thousand men should go and attack them. Don’t bother everyone else because Ai is small.”
\v 4 So around three thousand warriors went to \add attack Ai, but they were defeated\add* and had to flee \add back\add*.
\v 5 The men from Ai chased them from the \add city\add* gate downhill to Shevarim and fought them on the slopes. About thirty-six of them were killed, and the Israelites became very afraid and lost all their courage.
\p
\v 6 Joshua and the Israelite elders tore their clothes \add in grief\add* and fell to their knees in front of Yahweh’s box until evening, throwing dust onto their heads \add in despair\add*.
\v 7 Then Joshua asked, “Yahweh, my master, why did you bring these people across the Jordan and then let us be defeated and killed by the Amorites? If only we’d been content and stayed on the other side of the river.
\v 8 Please, my master, what can I say now that we have had to flee \add in shame\add* from our enemies?
\v 9 \add When\add* the Canaanites and the other people living in this land hear about this, they’ll surround us and kill all of us. Then what would you do to defend your honour?
\p
\v 10 But Yahweh told Joshua, “Stand up! Why is your face on the ground like that?
\v 11 Israel has sinned and disobeyed my agreement\f + \fr 7:11 \ft Probably referring to the ten commands written on the stone slabs.\f* that I made with them. They’ve also taken prohibited things—stealing, deceiving, and put it with their own possessions.
\v 12 \add That’s why\add* they were unable to defeat their enemies—they had to flee from them because they took what was prohibited. I won’t be with you all again unless you all destroy what was taken.
\v 13 \add So\add* get up and tell the people, ‘Purify yourselves for tomorrow, because our God Yahweh says that we took something prohibited and we won’t be able to better our enemies until it’s removed from among us.
\v 14 Tomorrow morning you all must present yourselves by your tribes. Then the tribe that Yahweh singles out, must present yourselves by your clans, and the clan that Yahweh singles out, must present yourselves by household. Then the household that Yahweh singles out, must present yourselves individually.
\v 15 The man who is caught will be destroyed by fire, along with everything that belongs to him, because he’s violated Yahweh’s agreement and committed a disgraceful sin among Israel.”
\p
\v 16 So Joshua got up early the next morning and ordered each tribe to come forward singly, and the Judah tribe was singled out.
\v 17 Then each of the clans came forward and the Zarhi clan was singled out, and when their families came forwards, Zavdi’s household was singled out.
\v 18 When everyone in that extended family came forward, Akan (son of Karmi, son of Zavdi, son of Zerah, from the Judah tribe) was singled out.
\v 19 Then Joshua told Akan, “My son, now honour Yahweh the god of Israel and give thanks to him. And tell me now what you’ve done—don’t hide it from me.”
\p
\v 20 “It’s true,” Akan replied, “I’ve sinned against Yahweh the god of Israel. This is what I did:
\v 21 I saw a beautiful Babylonian cloak and two hundred silver pieces and a tongue-shaped piece of gold (weighing six-hundred grams) \add there inside Jericho\add*. I wanted them so I took them, and they’re hidden in the ground under my tent, with the silver underneath.”
\p
\v 22 Joshua sent some messengers and they ran to the tent, and sure enough, it was all hidden there \add in the ground\add* with the silver underneath.
\v 23 They took it out of the tent and brought it back to Joshua and all the people, and spread it out in front of Yahweh.
\p
\v 24 Then Joshua took Akan and the silver, the cloak, the gold along with his children and his animals, his tent and everything he owned, and took them to the Akor valley along with all the people,
\v 25 and he said, “Why did you cause trouble for us? Yahweh will trouble you today.” And the people executed him by throwing rocks at him. Then they threw rocks at them \add all\add* and burnt \add the bodies\add*
\v 26 and piled rocks over them which are still there to this day and Yahweh’s anger subsided. That’s why that place is called ‘Akor’ \add (i.e., ‘Trouble’)\add* valley to this day.
\c 8
\s1 The defeat and destruction of Ai
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Joshua, “Don’t be afraid or discouraged. Take all your warriors with you and go to Ai. Listen, I’ve given you victory over Ai’s king and his people and his city and his land.
\v 2 You’ll do to Ai and its king what you did to Jericho and its king,\f + \fr 8:2 \ft We’re not actually given any details about what happened to the king of Jericho.\f* except that you all can plunder its spoil and its livestock for yourselves. Ambush the city from behind.”
\p
\v 3 So Joshua led his warriors towards Ai. He chose thirty thousand of the bravest and strongest warriors and sent them ahead in the darkness
\v 4 and told them, “Listen, you all lie in ambush against the city from close behind it, and make sure you all stay alert and ready \add to attack\add*.
\v 5 I will bring those warriors who are with me and we’ll approach the city, then when they come out to attack us like last time, we’ll run back away from them.
\v 6 They’ll think we’re running away from them like we did before, so they’ll chase after us until we’ve drawn them away from the city.
\v 7 Then you’ll all move in from your ambush \add position\add* and capture the city, and your god Yahweh will give the city to you all.
\v 8 After you capture the city, set fire to it. Do what I’ve ordered you all which in turn has come from Yahweh.
\v 9 So Joshua sent them to \add set up\add* the ambush between Ai and Bethel so west of Ai). Meanwhile, he stayed overnight at the camp.
\p
\v 10 Joshua got up early in the morning and gathered the warriors, then he and the Israelite elders went in front of them \add to go and attack\add* Ai.
\v 11 They all went close to the city and camped \add there slightly\add* to the north, with just a valley between them and Ai.
\v 12 (He had sent around five thousand warriors to lie in ambush between Bethel and Ai, west of the city,
\v 13 so the main group was to the north and the rear guard to the west.) That night Joshua went down into the valley.
\v 14 When the king of Ai saw the Israelites, he hurriedly got his men ready, and went out early to meet Israel for the battle. The king and his people went to a prepared spot overlooking the Jordan valley, but he didn’t know that another group lay in ambush behind the city.
\v 15 Joshua and the Israelite group allowed themselves to be struck by them, then they fled back towards the wilderness
\v 16 and all the men in the city were called out to chase them. So they chased after Joshua and were drawn away from the city,
\v 17 and there wasn’t a single man left in Ai or Bethel who didn’t join the chase—they left the city \add wide\add* open and chased after the Israelites.
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh told Joshua, “Hold your spear out pointing towards Ai, because I will allow you to defeat it.” So Joshua held the spear that was in his hand out toward the city.
\v 19 When the warriors in the ambush saw that, they rushed out from their place and entered the city and captured it, and quickly set it on fire.
\v 20 \add When\add* the men from Ai turned and looked behind them, the smoke from their city was going up into the sky, but they had nowhere to escape to. Then Joshua’s men who’d been fleeing towards the wilderness turned back around towards their pursuers
\v 21 and saw that the ambush had captured the city and the smoke was rising. So they continued back towards Ai, killing their men.
\v 22 Meanwhile, their warriors came \add back\add* out of the city, so the men from Ai ended up in the middle of the two groups, and the Israelites fought until they’d killed them all.
\v 23 However they captured the king of Ai alive and brought him to Joshua.
\p
\v 24 Then after the Israelites had finished killing the men of Ai who had chased them \add towards\add* the wilderness, they returned to Ai and slaughtered the people still in there,
\v 25 so a total of twelve thousand men and women from Ai were killed that day.
\v 26 Joshua didn’t lower his spear until everyone who lived in Ai had been completely destroyed.
\v 27 The Israelites only took the livestock and the goods from Ai, just as Yahweh had instructed Joshua,
\v 28 then Joshua burnt the city and made it a permanent pile of rubble where no one lives to this day.
\v 29 He hanged the king of Ai on a tree, but as the sun was going down, he commanded that the corpse be brought down from the tree. They threw it into the city gateway and piled a big heap of rocks on top of it which is still there today.
\s1 Reading Yahweh’s instructions at Mt. Eyval
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 30 Then Joshua built an altar to Israel’s god, Yahweh on Mt. Eyval
\v 31 just like Yahweh’s servant Moses had instructed them. (It was written in the scroll of the rules from Moses \add that it must be\add* an altar made from whole stones that haven’t been chiselled with iron tools.) Then they offered burnt offerings to Yahweh on it, plus they sacrificed peace offerings,
\v 32 and while the Israelites watched, Joshua inscribed onto the stones \add there\add*,\f + \fr 8:32 \ft Probably not the altar stones since an iron tool wasn’t allowed to be used on them.\f* a copy of the rules passed on by Moses.
\v 33 Then all of Israel and their elders and officials and judges were standing some on each side of the box containing Yahweh’s agreement, facing the priests and the Levites who carried the box. Any resident foreigners were also there. Half of the people stood on the Mt. Gerizim side, and half on the Mt. Eyval side \add of the valley\add*, just like \add the way that\add* Yahweh’s servant Moses had originally commanded for blessing the people.
\p
\v 34 Then Joshua read all the law, \add including\add* the blessings and the curses, as per everything that’s written in the book of the law.
\v 35 He read out every single word that Moses had written, in front of the entire assembly of Israel, including the women, children, and foreigners living among them.
\c 9
\s1 The Givonim deceive Joshua
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 There were several kings who ruled in regions on this west side of the Jordan —the Hittites and Amorites, the Canaanites and Perizim, the Hivim and the Jebusites. Some were in the hill country, some in the lowlands, and some along the Mediterranean coast south of Lebanon. \add When\add* they heard about what had happened at Ai,
\v 2 they formed an alliance to fight \add together\add* against Joshua and Israel.
\p
\v 3 However, when \add the people who\add* live in Gibeon heard what Joshua had done to Jericho and Ai,
\v 4 they decided to use cunning \add instead\add*. They found old, worn-out sacks to throw over their donkeys and old, worn-out wineskins that had been torn and mended, then sent men to act as ambassadors.
\v 5 They found worn-out sandals that had been patched, and wore worn-out clothes. All the food that they took for the journey was already dry and disintegrating into crumbs.
\v 6 They went to Joshua in the camp at Gilgal and told him, “We’ve come from a far-off land and want you to make a treaty with us.”
\p
\v 7 An Israelite man asked the Hivi \add (because Gilead was in the Hivi region)\add*, “Perhaps you \add really\add* live near by? How could we make a treaty with you?”
\p
\v 8 They addressed Joshua, “We are your servants.”
\p Joshua asked them, “Who are you? And where have you come from?”
\p
\v 9 “Your servants have come from a very distant land,” they replied, “because of the reputation of your god, Yahweh. We’ve heard the reports about him and everything that he did in Egypt,
\v 10 and everything that he did to the two Amorite kings on the other side of the Jordan—King Sihon of Heshbon, and King Og of the Bashan who lived in Ashtarot.
\v 11 So our elders and all those who live in our area instructed us, ‘Take provisions for the journey and go to meet them, and tell them, “We are your servants. Now make a treaty with us.” ’
\v 12 This bread of ours was warm from our ovens when we loaded it for our journey, and now, look, it’s \add all\add* dry and crumbling.
\v 13 And these wineskins were new when we filled them, and look, \add now\add* they’re \add all\add* torn. Same with our clothes and our sandals—they’re \add all\add* worn-out from the very long journey.”
\p
\v 14 The Israelite leaders tasted some of their provisions, but they didn’t ask Yahweh for advice.
\v 15 So Joshua agreed to make peace with them, and they made a treaty not to attack their people, and all the Israelite leaders promised that.
\p
\v 16 However, three days after making the treaty, they discovered that those people actually lived quite close to where they were.
\v 17 Then the Israelites went on forward and they arrived at those cities on the third day—the cities of Gibeon, Kefirah, Be’erot, and Kiryat-Yearim.
\v 18 But they didn’t attack those cities because their leaders had made a promise by the god of Israel, Yahweh.
\p All the Israelites grumbled against their leaders for making that treaty,
\v 19 but their leaders told them, “We ourselves have promised them by our God Yahweh so now we can’t touch them.
\v 20 We’re forced now to let them live, otherwise God’s anger would be against us for breaking our promise to them.
\v 21 So we’ll let them live \add but make them our slaves\add*.” As a result of that, the Givonim became woodcutters and water-carriers for all the Israelites.
\p
\v 22 Then Joshua summoned them and asked them, “Why did you all deceive us by telling us that you lived far away when really you were very close?
\v 23 Because of that, you’re all cursed and you’ll always be our slaves—you’ll all be the woodcutters and water-carriers for the house of my god.”
\p
\v 24 “Because it was made very clear to your servants,” they answered, “that your god Yahweh had commanded his servant Moses to give to you all the land and to destroy everyone who lives in the region. We feared for our lives when you came closer, so that’s what we did.
\v 25 So now we’re in your power—do to us whatever you think is good and right.”
\v 26 So that’s what Joshua did and he prevented the Israelites from killing them.
\v 27 Instead he made them move to where he wanted, to become woodcutters and water-carriers for the people and for Yahweh’s altar until this day.
\c 10
\s1 The defeat of the Amorites
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Once King Adoni-Tsedek of Jerusalem heard that Joshua had captured Ai and completely destroyed it, and that he’d treated the king of Ai the same as he had treated the king of Jericho, and he heard that the Givonim had made peace with Israel and were \add now working for\add* them,
\v 2 the people of Jerusalem were very afraid because Gibeon had been a powerful city, stronger than Ai, and all its warriors were powerful.
\v 3 So King Adoni-Tsedek sent to King Hoham of Hebron, King Pir’am of Yarmut, King Yafia of Lakish, and King Devir of Eglon, saying,
\v 4 “Come here and help me. We’ll attack Gibeon because they’ve made peace with Joshua and the Israelites.”
\v 5 So those five Amorite kings (from Jerusalem, Hebron, Yarmut, Lakish, and Eglon) joined together to go to Gibeon and their \add armies\add* camped there to fight against it.
\p
\v 6 The leaders of Gibeon \add hurriedly\add* sent to Joshua in the camp at Gilgal, saying, “Don’t abandon your servants. Come quickly and help us, and save us because all the kings of the Amorites who live in the hill country have combined against us.”
\v 7 So Joshua and his warriors and prize fighters headed off from Gilgal,
\v 8 and Yahweh told him, “Don’t be afraid of them, because you’ll have the victory over them—none of them will be able to stand against you.”
\p
\v 9 Joshua \add and his men\add* spent the night moving in, and then surprised the alliance,
\v 10 and Yahweh confused them as the Israelites approached and \add caused them\add* to be badly defeated at Gibeon. Then they chased them on the road going to Beyt-Horon and killed them as far as Azekah and Makedah.
\v 11 As they fled from the Israelites on the slope down from Beyt-Horon heading towards Azekah, Yahweh hurled large hailstones down on them from the sky and they died—\add actually\add* more of them died from the hail than were killed with swords by the Israelites.
\p
\v 12 Then Joshua spoke to Yahweh on the day that he gave them victory over the Amorites, and in front of the Israelites he said,
\q1 “Sun, stay still over Gibeon,
\q1 and moon, over the Ayalon valley.
\m
\v 13 Then the sun stood still and the moon stood still until the nation had avenged themselves of their enemies. (Isn’t that written about in the Jashar scroll? The sun stood still in the middle of the sky and it didn’t hurry to go around for almost a whole day.
\v 14 There had never before been a day like that, and nothing like it since then, when Yahweh listened to a man’s voice, because he was fighting for Israel.)
\p
\v 15 Then Joshua and all Israel returned to their camp at Gilgal.
\s1 Five Amorite kings defeated
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 16 Meanwhile those five kings had fled away and hidden at Makedah in a cave,
\v 17 and Joshua was told, “The five kings have been found hiding in the cave at Makedah.”
\v 18 Joshua said, “Roll large rocks across the mouth of the cave and assign men to guard them there,
\v 19 but \add the rest of you,\add* don’t stay there. Chase after your enemies and attack them from behind. Don’t let them get into their cities, because your god Yahweh wants you all to defeat them.
\v 20 So Joshua and the Israelites finished most of them off, although some did manage to escape and get back into their fortified cities.
\v 21 Then all the people returned to Joshua in the camp at Makedah. \add After that,\add* no one dared to criticise the Israelites.
\p
\v 22 Then Joshua said, “Open up the cave and bring those five kings to me.”
\v 23 They did it and brought the five kings out from the cave—the kings of Jerusalem, Hebron, Yarmut, Lakish, and Eglon.
\v 24 When they took them to Joshua, he summoned all the warriors and told the commanders, “Come over here. Put your feet on the necks of these kings.”
\v 25 Then he told them, “Don’t be afraid and don’t be dismayed. Be strong and courageous, because this is what Yahweh will do to all your enemies—including the ones that you all still have to fight.”
\v 26 Then Joshua executed the five kings and hanged them on five trees, where their bodies were left until evening.
\v 27 As the sun was going down, he commanded that the bodies be brought down and thrown into the cave where they had hidden, and they placed large rocks over the cave entrance \add again, which are still there\add* to this day.
\s1 Joshua attacks more Amorites
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 28 Then Joshua captured Makedah that \add same\add* day. They killed the king and everyone else in the city, leaving no survivors, and Joshua did the same to the king of Makedah as he’d done to the king of Jericho.\f + \fr 10:28 \ft We’re not actually given any details about what happened to the king of Jericho.\f*
\p
\v 29 Then Joshua and all Israel went \add southwest\add* from Makedah to Livnah and fought against it.
\v 30 Yahweh also allowed Israel to conquer it and its king, and Joshua \add again\add* killed everyone in the city, leaving no survivors, and he did the same to the king of Livnah as he’d done to the king of Jericho.
\p
\v 31 Then Joshua and all Israel went \add south\add* from Livnah to Lakish, where they took a position against the city and fought against it.
\v 32 On the second day \add of the battle\add*, Yahweh helped Israel defeat Lakish, and they captured it and killed everyone in the city like they had done to Livnah.
\v 33 King Horam of Gezer arrived to help Lakish, but Joshua defeated him and his men, leaving no survivors.
\p
\v 34 Then Joshua and the Israelites went \add west\add* from Lakish to Eglon, where they took a position against the city and fought against it.
\v 35 That day they captured it and killed everyone in the city like they had done to Lakish.
\p
\v 36 Then Joshua and the Israelites went \add west\add* from Eglon up \add into the hills\add* to Hebron, and fought against it.
\v 37 They captured it and killed the king and everyone in all their towns, leaving no survivors the same as Eglon.
\p
\v 38 Then Joshua and the Israelites went to Devir \add city\add* and fought against it.
\v 39 They captured the city and its king and all their towns, killing everyone and leaving no survivors. They treated Devir and its king just like they had to Livnah and Hebron and their kings.
\p
\v 40 So Joshua attacked and conquered the entire region—the hill country and the Negev, the lowlands and the slopes, and all their kings. He didn’t leave any survivors but destroyed everything living, just as Israel’s god Yahweh had commanded.
\v 41 Joshua attacked and conquered them from Kadesh-Barnea and as far as Gaza, all of Goshen and as far as Gibeon.
\v 42 In one swoop, Joshua captured all those kings and their land, because Israel’s god Yahweh had fought for Israel.
\v 43 Then Joshua and his warriors returned to their camp at Gilgal.
\c 11
\s1 Joshua defeats the northern kings
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Now when King Yavin of Hatsor heard about all that, he sent to King Yovav of Madon and the kings of Shimron and Akshaf,
\v 2 as well as the kings from the hill country in the north and in the rift valley plains south of Galilee \add lake\add*, and in the lowlands and in the heights of Dor in the west,
\v 3 and the kings of the Canaanites both east and west, and the Amorites, Hittites, Perizim, and the Jebusites who lived in the hill country, and to the Hivim by \add Mt.\add* Hermon in the Mitspah region.
\v 4 So they \add all\add* came out with their warriors—so many people that they were as many as the \add grains of\add* sand on the seashore, along with many horses and chariots.
\v 5 All those kings gathered together at the time they’d specified and took up positions at the Merom stream to fight against Israel.
\p
\v 6 But Yahweh told Joshua, “Don’t be afraid that they look overwhelming, because tomorrow at this time, I’ll ensure that they’re all slain by Israel. You’ll hamstring their horses and burn their chariots.”
\v 7 Then Joshua and all his warriors went to Merom and attacked them without warning
\v 8 and Yahweh enabled them to defeat them.
\v 9 Then Joshua did what Yahweh had instructed him—he crippled their horses and set fire to their chariots.
\p
\v 10 After that, Joshua returned to Hatsor \add city\add* and captured it and killed the king—in the past it had been the head of all those kingdoms.
\v 11 They killed everything in the city—not leaving anything that breathes—and then set fire to it.
\p
\v 12 So Joshua \add had\add* captured all those kings and their cities, and completely destroyed them and their people just as Yahweh’s servant Moses had commanded.
\v 13 \add However,\add* other than Hatsor, they didn’t burn any of the \add other more defensible\add* cities built \add up\add* on mounds.
\v 14 The Israelites took any valuables and any livestock from those cities, but they killed everyone and everything else.
\v 15 Joshua did everything exactly as Yahweh had instructed his servant Moses (and as Moses had then instructed him), not forgetting or ignoring anything.
\s1 Joshua controls the entire region
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 16 Joshua conquered the whole region—the hill country and all the Negev, all of the Goshen region, the lowlands and the rift valley plains, the Israel hill country and its lowlands
\v 17 from Mt. Halak downhill towards Se’ir, and as far as Baal-Gad in the Lebanon valley below Mt. Hermon. He attacked and captured all their kings and executed them.
\v 18 Joshua had spent quite some time battling with those kings.
\v 19 No city made peace with the Israelites except for the Hivim who lived in Gibeon—all the rest were taken in battle
\v 20 because it was Yahweh who’d made them stubborn so they’d fight against Israel and be completely destroyed without any mercy. \add Yes,\add* they were annihilated just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 21 At that time, Joshua \add also\add* went and exterminated the Anakim from the hill country, from Hebron, Devir, Anav, and all the Judah hill country and Israel. He completely destroyed them from their cities.
\v 22 No descendants of Anak were left in Israel, except that \add some\add* remained in Gaza, in Gath, and in Ashdod.
\p
\v 23 Joshua captured the entire region, just as Yahweh had told Moses, and Joshua gave it as an inheritance to Israel, divided into portions according to their tribes, and then the region was peaceful.
\c 12
\s1 The kings defeated by Moses
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 These are the kings in the region who the Israelites defeated on the eastern side of the Jordan \add river\add* and took possession of their land from the Arnon riverbed \add in the south\add* to Mt. Hermon \add in the north\add*, including all of the eastern rift valley plains:
\v 2 King Sihon of the Amorites who lived in Heshbon and ruled \add over\add* Aroer (on the edge of the Arnon riverbed) and \add over\add* half of Gilead up to the Yabok riverbed \add which formed\add* the border with the Ammonites,
\v 3 as well as the eastern rift valley plains down to Galilee lake and to the Aravah sea (\add also called\add* the Dead sea), the road to Beyt-Hayeshimot and the area southward below the slopes of \add Mt.\add* Pisgah.
\p
\v 4 \add The Israelites also defeated\add* King Og from Bashan, one of the few remaining Refaim, who lived in Ashtarot and at Edre’i,
\v 5 and ruled over Mt. Hermon, Salkah, all of Bashan to the border of the Geshurim and Ma’akatim, as well as half of Gilead to the border with King Sihon from Heshbon.
\p
\v 6 Yahweh’s servant Moses and the Israelites had defeated them and Moses had given that region as a possession to the \add descendants of\add* Reuben and Gad and to the Manasseh half-tribe.
\s1 The kings defeated by Joshua
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 7 These are the kings in the region that Joshua and the Israelites defeated on the western side of the Jordan \add river\add* from Baal-Gad in the Lebanon valley to Mt. Halak which goes up toward Se’ir. Joshua divided up that region as a possession to the \add various\add* tribes of Israel,
\v 8 as well as the hill country, the lowlands, the rift valley, the slopes, the wilderness and the Negev—\add the land of\add* the Hittites, Amorites, Canaanites, Perizim, Hivim, and the Jebusites:
\v 9 the king of Jericho, the king of Ai (near Bethel,
\v 10 the king of Jerusalem, the king of Hebron,
\v 11 the king of Yarmut, the king of Lakish,
\v 12 the king of Eglon, the king of Gezer,
\v 13 the king of Devir, the king of Geder,
\v 14 the king of Hormah, the king of Arad,
\v 15 the king of Livnah, the king of Adulam,
\v 16 the king of Makedah, the king of Bethel,
\v 17 the king of Tapuah, the king of Hefer,
\v 18 the king of Afek, the king of Lasharon,
\v 19 the king of Madon, the king of Hatsor,
\v 20 the king of Shimron Meron, the king of Akshaf,
\v 21 the king of Ta’anak, the king of Megiddo,
\v 22 the king of Kedesh, the king of Yakeneam in Carmel,
\v 23 the king of Dor of Nafoth Dor, the king of Goyim in Gilgal,
\v 24 and the king of Tirtsah—a total of thirty-one kings.
\c 13
\s1 The regions not yet captured
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Now the years had passed and Joshua had grown old, and Yahweh said to him, “You’re old \add now\add* and there’s still a lot of land that needs to be captured.
\v 2 This is the remaining land: all the territories of the Philistines, and all of the Geshurim,
\v 3 from Shihor (east of Egypt and as far as the border of Ekron to the north (it’s considered to be Canaanite, the five rulers of the Philistines (in Gaza, Ashdod, Ashkelon, Gath, and Ekron), and the Avim.
\v 4 In the south: all the land of the Canaanites, and a Me’arah that \add belongs to\add* the Sidonians, as far as Afek, as far as the border of the Amorites,
\v 5 plus the land of the Givlim, all Lebanon towards the east, from Baal-Gad below Mt. Hermon as far as Levo-Hamat.
\v 6 I myself will drive out all those who live in the hill country from Lebanon as far as Misrefot-Mayim, and all the Sidonians. Make sure that it’s given to the Israelites as an inheritance, just as I instructed you.
\v 7 All that land should be divided up as an inheritance for the nine tribes plus \add half of\add* the Manasseh half-tribe.
\s1 Division of the land east of the Jordan
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 8 Along with the other \add half of\add* the Manasseh half-tribe, the Reuben and Gad tribes had taken their inheritance on the other (eastern) side of the Jordan as approved by Yahweh’s servant Moses,
\v 9 from Aroer, which is on the edge of the Arnon riverbed, and the city that’s in the middle of the valley, and all the plateau near Meydeva, to Divon.
\v 10 \add Also,\add* all the cities of King Sihon of the Amorites who ruled in Heshbon, as far as the border with the Ammonites,
\v 11 and Gilead, and the territory of the Geshurim and the Ma’akatim, and all Mt. Hermon, and all the Bashan as far as Salkah,
\v 12 all Og’s kingdom in the Bashan, who reigned in Ashtarot and in Edre’i (what remained from the remnant of the Refaim). Moses had attacked them and captured their lands.
\v 13 But the Israelites didn’t capture the land of the Geshurim or the Ma’akatim and so those peoples live among the Israelites to this day.
\p
\v 14 The only tribe that didn’t receive an inheritance was the Levi tribe because the offerings given to Israel’s god Yahweh are their inheritance, just like he’d told them.
\s1 The area assigned to Reuben’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 15 This was the land that Moses gave to the Reuben tribe, according to their clans:
\v 16 The area from Aroer (on the edge of the Arnon riverbed) along to the town in the middle of the valley, to the plateau near Meydeva,
\v 17 Heshbon and all its cities on the plateau, \add including\add* Divon, Bamot-Baal, Beyt-Baal-Me’on,
\v 18 Yahats, Kedemot, Mofa’at,
\v 19 Kiryatayim, Sivmah, and Tseret-Hashahar on the hill within the valley.
\v 20 \add It also includes\add* Beyt-Pe’or, the slopes of the Pisgah, Beyt-Hayeshimot,
\v 21 and all the cities on the plateau, and all the kingdom of Sihon (the king of the Amorites who had reigned in Heshbon) who Moses had defeated, he and the leaders of Midian: Evi and Rekem and Tsur and Hur and Reva (the princes of Sihon who had lived in the land).
\v 22 The Israelites also killed Balaam Beor’s son who practiced divination) along with others.
\v 23 The border for Reuben’s descendants was the Jordan \add river\add*. So that was the inheritance of Reuben’s descendants—all the cities and their villages allocated to their clans.
\s1 The area assigned to Gad’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 24 This was the land that Moses gave to the Gad tribe, according to their clans:
\v 25 It included Yazer and all the cities of Gilead and half of the land where the Ammonites had lived as far as Aroer opposite Rabah,
\v 26 then from Heshbon to Ramat-Hamitspeh and Betonim, and from Mahanayim to the Devir border.
\v 27 Also in the valley, Beyt-Haram, Beyt-Nimrah, Sukot, Tsafon, and the rest of the kingdom of Sihon (the king of Heshbon), with the Jordan and the border, as far as the end of Galilee lake, eastward beyond the Jordan \add river\add*.
\v 28 So that was the inheritance of Gad’s descendants—all the cities and their villages allocated to their clans.
\s1 The area assigned to Manasseh’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 29 This was the land that Moses gave to half of the Manasseh half-tribe, according to their clans:
\v 30 It went from Mahanayim, all the Bashan, all the kingdom of Og (the king of the Bashan), and all the towns of Yair, which are in the Bashan—sixty cities.
\v 31 Also half of the Gilead, plus Ashtarot and Edre’i (cities of Og’s kingdom in the Bashan). So that was for half of Makir’s sons (he was one of Manasseh’s sons) allocated to their clans.
\p
\v 32 This was the land that Moses gave as an inheritance in the Moab plains on the eastern side of the Jordan \add river opposite\add* Jericho.
\v 33 However, Moses didn’t give any inheritance \add of land\add* to Levi’s descendants \add because\add* Israel’s god Yahweh is their inheritance just as he told them.
\c 14
\s1 The division of the main block west of the Jordan
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 These are what the Israelites inherited in Canaan, which Eleazar the priest and Joshua Nun’s son) and the leaders of the Israelites gave them as an inheritance.
\v 2 Their allocations were made by casting lots for the nine and a half tribes, just as Yahweh had commanded through Moses.
\v 3 (Moses had given the other two and a half tribes their inheritance on the other side of the river, but the Levites weren’t given land as an inheritance—
\v 4 instead of land they were given cities to live in, along with the \add surrounding\add* fields for their livestock. Joseph’s two sons Manasseh and Ephraim were considered as two tribes.)
\v 5 The Israelites did just as Yahweh had commanded Moses—they allocated blocks of the land \add as permanent possessions\add*.
\s1 Hebron is allocated to Caleb
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 6 Leaders from the Judah \add tribe\add* approached Joshua at Gilgal and Caleb son of Yefuneh the Kenizi) said to him, “You yourself know what Yahweh told the man of God Moses at Kadesh-Barnea about me and you.
\v 7 I was forty years old when Yahweh’s servant Moses sent me from Kadesh-Barnea to spy out the land, and I brought back an honest report.
\v 8 The other men who went made the people terrified, but I remained loyal to my god Yahweh.
\v 9 Moses promised me that day, saying, ‘The land that you walked on will certainly be for an inheritance for you and your sons forever, because you were completely loyal to my god Yahweh.’
\v 10 Now, listen. Yahweh has kept me alive these forty-five years since Yahweh said that to Moses, \add back\add* when Israel was walking in the wilderness. Yes, believe it or not, I’m now eighty-five.
\v 11 I’m still as strong today as \add I was\add* on the day that Moses sent me. Just like I could fight back then, I can still fight or travel just the same.
\v 12 So now, please give me that hill country that Yahweh promised that day, because you yourself heard me say that the Anakim lived there in fortified cities. \add So now,\add* perhaps Yahweh will help me to take them over just as he said.”
\p
\v 13 So Joshua blessed Yefuneh’s son Caleb and gave him Hebron as his inheritance.
\v 14 Because of that, Hebron belongs to Caleb (Yefuneh the Kenizi’s son) as an inheritance until this day, because he was completely loyal to Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 15 (Hebron used to be called Kiryat-Arba—Arba had been a brave warrior among the Anakim.)
\p Then there was a time of no more war in that region.
\c 15
\s1 The area assigned to Judah’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 \add This was the land\add* that was assigned to Judah’s descendants according to their clans:
\p It went from the Zin wilderness in the south to the border with Edom,
\v 2 and their southern border was the south-facing bay at the end of the Dead sea.
\v 3 \add From there\add* it went south of the Scorpion Pass, across to Zin, climbed up to Kadesh-Barnea, crossed to Hezron, went to Adar, and around to Karka.
\v 4 Then it crosses to Atsmon and out to the Egyptian riverbed and finishes at the \add Mediterranean\add* sea. That will be their southern border.
\p
\v 5 The eastern border was the Dead sea as far as the end of the Jordan.
\p The northern border \add starts\add* at the top of the \add Dead\add* sea where the Jordan \add river\add* enters it
\v 6 then to Beyt-Hoglah and \add further\add* north to Beyt-Ha’aravah to the Stone of Bohan (Reuben’s son).
\v 7 Then the border went to Devir from the Akor valley, and to the north, turning to the Gilgal, which is in front of the Adumim pass which is from the south of the riverbed, then the border passed over to the waters of Eyn-Shemesh and it ends at Eyn-Rogel.
\v 8 From there the border went up the Ben-Hinom valley to the edge of the Jebusite region from the south (that is Jerusalem. Then the border went to the head of the hill opposite the Hinom valley to the west, which is at the end of the Refaim valley to the north.
\v 9 From there the border turned from the head of the hill to the Neftoah spring and went out to the cities of Mt. Efron, then the border turned to Ba’alah (now called Kiryat-Yearim).
\v 10 Then the border went around from Ba’alah to the west to Mt. Se’ir, and passed over to the shoulder of Mt. Yearim from the north (also called Kesalon), and went down to Beyt-Shemesh, and passed over to Timnah.
\v 11 From there the border went out to the shoulder of Ekron to the north, then turned toward Shikeron and passed over to Mt. Ba’alah, and went out to Yavne’el, then the border ended at the sea.
\v 12 The western border was the Mediterranean sea. Those were the borders of \add the land given to\add* Judah’s descendants to be allocated to their clans.
\s1 Caleb defeats Hebron and Devir
\s4 Promised land
\r (Jdg. 1:11-15)
\p
\v 13 Yahweh had instructed Joshua to give \add this\add* block of land to Caleb Yefuneh’s son): Kiryat-Arba (now called Hebron—Arba was Anak’s father).
\v 14 Caleb captured \add the land\add* from Anak’s three sons—Sheshay, Ahiman, and Talmay,
\v 15 then from there, he battled against Devir (formerly called Kiryat-Sefer)
\v 16 and he said, “I’ll give my daughter Aksah to marry to the man who attacks Kiryat-Sefer and captures it.”
\v 17 Then Otniel (Kenaz’s son and Caleb’s brother) captured the city, so Caleb gave his daughter Aksah to him for a wife.
\v 18 Then when she married him, she encouraged him to ask her father for a field, so she got down off her donkey and Caleb asked, “What are you up to?”
\v 19 “Give me a blessing,” she answered, “because you’ve given me land in the Negev \add desert region\add*, so now give me some springs \add as well\add* for water.” So he gave her \add both\add* the upper and the lower springs.
\s1 Cities captured by Judah’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 20 This is Judah’s inheritance according to their clans:
\v 21 The cities at the edge of Judah’s block toward the border with Edom in Negev were Kavtse’el, Eder, Yagur,
\v 22 Kinah, Dimonah, Ad’adah,
\v 23 Kedesh, Hatsor, Yitnan,
\v 24 Zif, Telem, Be’alot,
\v 25 Hatsor-Hadatah, Keriyot-Hezron (also called Hatsor),
\v 26 Amam, Shema, Moladah,
\v 27 Hatsar-Gadah, Heshmon, Beyt-Pelet,
\v 28 Hatsar-Shual, Beersheba, and Bizyoteyah,
\v 29 Ba’alah, Iyim, Etsem,
\v 30 Eltolad, Kesil, Hormah,
\v 31 Tsiklag, Madmanah, Sansanah,
\v 32 Leva’ot, Shilhim, Ayin, and Rimon—a total of twenty-nine cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 33 \add Then down\add* in the lowlands were Eshtaol, Tsor’ah, Ashnah,
\v 34 Zanoah, Eyn-Ganim, Tapuah, Eynam,
\v 35 Yarmut, Adulam, Sokoh, Azekah,
\v 36 Sha’arayim, Aditayim, and Gederah (\add also called\add* Gederotayim)—a total of fourteen cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\p
\v 37 \add Also\add* Tsenan, Hadashah, Migdal-Gad,
\v 38 Dil’an, Mitspah, Yakete’el,
\v 39 Lakish, Batsekat, Eglon,
\v 40 Kabon, Lahmam, Kitlish,
\v 41 Gederot, Beyt-Dagon, Na’amah, and Makedah—sixteen cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 42 \add Also\add* Livnah, Eter, Ashan,
\v 43 Jephthah, Ashnah, Netsiv,
\v 44 Ke’ilah, Akziv, and Mareshah—nine cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 45 \add Also\add* Ekron with its towns and villages
\v 46 from Ekron to the Mediterranean—those near Ashdod and their villages.
\p
\v 47 \add Also\add* Ashdod and its towns and its villages, Gaza and its towns and its villages, as far as the riverbed of Egypt and the Mediterranean sea and the border.
\p
\v 48 \add Also\add* in the hill country: Shamir, Yatir, Sokoh,
\v 49 Danah, Kiryat-Sanah (also called Devir),
\v 50 Anav, Eshtemoa, Anim,
\v 51 Goshen, Holon, and Giloh—eleven cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 52 \add Also\add* Aravi, Dumah, Esh’an,
\v 53 Yanum, Beyt-Tapuah, Afekah,
\v 54 Humtah, and Kiryat-Arba (also called Hebron) and Tsi’or—nine cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 55 \add Also\add* Maeon, Carmel, Zif, Yutah,
\v 56 Jezreel, Yakedeam, Zanoah,
\v 57 Cain, Giveah, and Timnah—ten cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 58 \add Also\add* Halhul, Beyt-Tsur, Gedor,
\v 59 Ma’arat, Beyt-Anot, and Eltekon—six cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 60 \add Also\add* Kiryat-Baal (also called Kiryat-Yearim) and Rabah—two cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 61 In the wilderness, Beyt-Ha’aravah, Midin, Sekakah,
\v 62 Nivshan, the Ir-Hamelah, and Eyn-Gedi—six cities as well as their villages.
\p
\v 63 But Judah’s descendants weren’t able to defeat the Jebusites who lived in Jerusalem, so they have lived among the Judah tribe to this day.
\c 16
\s1 The allocation to Joseph’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 The block for Joseph’s descendants\f + \fr 16:1 \ft i.e., the Ephraim and Manasseh half-tribes.\f* went from the Jordan at Jericho \add Jericho\add*, east of the springs), through the wilderness into the Bethel hill country.
\v 2 It went out from Bethel to Luz then along the border with the Arkim at Atarot.
\v 3 Then it went west to the border with the Yafletites as far as the border with Lower Beyt-Horon and as far as Gezer, then out to the Mediterranean.
\p
\v 4 So Joseph’s sons Manasseh and Ephraim received an inheritance.
\s1 The allocation to Ephraim’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 5 The allotment for Ephraim’s descendants according to their clans was at Atrot-Adar in the east, going as far as Upper Beyt-Horon
\v 6 and out to the Mediterranean. From Mikmetat in the north, the border went eastward toward Ta’anat-Shiloh then further east to Yanoah.
\v 7 From Yanoah it went to Atarot and Na’arah, reached Jericho then came out at the Jordan.
\v 8 \add The northern border went\add* from Tapuah to the Kanah riverbed and then out to the Mediterranean. That was the inheritance of Ephraim’s descendants according to their clans,
\v 9 although some of the cities with their villages for Ephraim’s descendants were \add actually\add* inside the territory assigned to Manasseh’s descendants.
\v 10 But they didn’t capture the land of the Canaanites who lived in Gezer, so they’ve lived within Ephraim to this day and were forced to serve as labourers.
\c 17
\s1 The allocation to Manasseh’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 The allotment for Manasseh’s descendants (Joseph’s eldest son) \add included a special allotment for\add* (Gilead’s father) Makir—he was given Gilead and Bashan because he was a powerful warrior.
\v 2 The other Manasseh clans \add that were allocated land\add* were the Aviezer, Helek, Asriel, Shechem, Hefer, and Shemida clans. (They were the sons of Joseph’s son Manasseh \add who led their own\add* clans.)
\v 3 But Tselofehad (son of Hefer, son of Gilead, son of Makir, son of Manasseh) had no sons—only daughters who were named Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milkah, and Tirtsah.
\v 4 They approached the priest Eleazar and Joshua and the other leaders and said, “Yahweh told Moses to give us an inheritance along with our brothers.” So he\f + \fr 17:4 \ft It’s not clear who this ‘he’ refers to here—some translations choose Eleazar (implied by the sentence structure) and others choose Joshua (implied by past allocations of land).\f* gave them an inheritance (like he’d given their uncles), as Yahweh had instructed them.
\v 5 So ten blocks were allocated to Manasseh \add tribe\add* as well as Gilead and Bashan which were across the Jordan
\v 6 because Manasseh’s daughters received inheritances along with his sons. Gilead belonged to the rest of Manasseh’s sons.
\p
\v 7 The border for Manasseh went from Asher to Mikmetat opposite Shechem, then went south towards the spring at Tapuah.
\v 8 (The Tapuah \em land\em* was for Manasseh, but Tapuah \add town\add* was for Ephraim.)
\v 9 Then the border went south to the Kanah riverbed—the cities there belonged to Ephraim even though they were inside Manasseh’s land—then the border stayed on the north side of the riverbed and extended to the Mediterranean.
\v 10 \add So\add* the south was for Ephraim and the north was for Manasseh, and the sea was the \add western\add* border, meeting Asher from the north and Issachar from the east.
\v 11 Manasseh was allocated \add cities\add* inside \add both\add* Issachar’s and Asher’s \add territories\add*: Beyt-Shean, Yivleam, Dor, Eyn-Dor, Ta’anak, and Megiddo along with their surrounding towns.\f + \fr 17:11 \ft We’ve omitted the final Hebrew phrase here (the three of Nafet) since it’s unclear what it means (plus it’s not considered to be a vital point in the narrative).\f*
\v 12 But Manasseh’s descendants weren’t able to take possession of those cities, so the Canaanites continued to live in the region.
\v 13 When the Israelites strengthened their position, they were able to force those Canaanites to work for them, but they weren’t able to drive them out.
\s1 Ephraim and Manasseh’s descendants are given additional land
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 14 Then the \add two\add* Ephraim and Manasseh half-tribes asked Joshua, “Why have you \add only\add* given to us one measured allocation as an inheritance when we’re \add very\add* numerous \add because\add* Yahweh has already blessed us?”
\p
\v 15 “If there’s so many of you,” Joshua answered, “and if Ephraim’s hill-country is too small for you, go into the forested area of the Perizim and the Refaim and clear some land for yourselves there.”
\p
\v 16 “The hill country \add certainly\add* isn’t big enough for \add all of\add* us,” they responded, “plus the Canaanites living in the Jezreel valley and in Beyt-Shean and the surrounding towns have iron chariots.”
\p
\v 17 So Joshua told the Ephraim and Manasseh half-tribes, “\add Yes\add*, there’s a lot of you and you’re very powerful. One land allotment wouldn’t be enough for you
\v 18 so the hill country will be yours \add as well\add*. However, it’s a forest and you’ll have to clear it to the borders. You’ll \add need to\add* drive out the Canaanites even if they are strong and with iron chariots.”
\c 18
\s1 Allocations for the remaining seven tribes
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then all the Israelites assembled at Shiloh and set up the \add sacred\add* tent there. The battles over the land had stopped
\v 2 but seven of their tribes hadn’t had their inheritances assigned \add yet\add*.
\v 3 Joshua asked the Israelites, “How much longer are you all going to put off capturing the land that your ancestors’ God Yahweh has given you?
\v 4 Choose three men from each \add of those seven\add*\f + \fr 18:4 \ft Not in the Hebrew but implied by verse 5 as they go on to divide the surveyed land into seven portions.\f* tribes and I’ll send them out to scout around \add and inspect\add* the land and write descriptions of it in terms of potential allocations and bring it to me.
\v 5 They can divide that land into seven parts. (Judah will maintain their territory in the south, and Joseph’s tribes in the north.)
\v 6 You all should write descriptions of the seven parts and bring them here to me, and in front of our god Yahweh, I’ll throw dice for you \add to allocate them to the seven tribes\add*.
\v 7 However, the Levites don’t receive a portion, because their inheritance is Yahweh’s priesthood. Gad and Reuben and the Manasseh half-tribe have \add already\add* received their inheritance that Yahweh’s servant Moses allocated to them on the eastern side of the Jordan.
\p
\v 8 So the \add chosen\add* men got ready to leave and Joshua instructed them, “Go and walk around that land and write \add a report\add* and bring it back to me. Then I’ll throw dice for you all in front of Yahweh here in Shiloh.”
\v 9 Then the men left and walked through the area, writing down \add their recommendations\add* for dividing the seven portions according to the cities \add in the area\add*, then they returned back to Joshua at the camp at Shiloh.
\v 10 So Joshua threw dice for them at Shiloh in front of Yahweh, and so that land was allocated to those remaining seven tribes.
\s1 The allocation to Benjamin’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 11 The Benjamin tribe won the \add first\add* draw and the area for their clans was between the land assigned to Judah and that assigned to Ephraim and Manasseh.
\v 12 Their northern border went from the Jordan then to the northern side of Jericho and up into the hill country to the west, coming out in the wilderness at Beyt-Aven.
\v 13 Then it went across towards Luz (now called Bethel to the southern edge then downhill towards Atrot-Adar beside the hill south of lower Beyt-Horon.
\v 14 Then the western border turned from that hill and went south, coming out at Kiryat-Baal (also called Kiryat-Yearim—a town belonging to Judah) thus forming the western side.
\v 15 The southern border went west from Kiryat-Yearim to the spring at Neftoah,
\v 16 then downhill to the Ben-Hinom valley (in the Refaim valley to the north) then downhill to Hinom valley to the edge of the Jebusites and downhill to Eyn-Rogel.
\v 17 Then it turned northward and went \add west\add* to Eyn-Shemesh and across to Gelilot opposite the Adumim pass, before going downhill to the Stone of Bohan (Reuben’s son).
\v 18 It extended over to the northern slope of the Jordan valley and then downhill into the \add actual\add* valley.
\v 19 It then went \add east\add* to the northern slope of Beyt-Hoglah before coming out at the north end of the Dead sea where the Jordan \add river\add* flows into it. That was the southern boundary
\v 20 and the Jordan borders it on the east. Those were the borders of Benjamin’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans.
\p
\v 21 The cities allocated to Benjamin’s descendants and their clans were: Jericho, Beyt-Hoglah, Emek-Ketsits,
\v 22 Beyt-Ha’aravah, Tsemarayim, Bethel,
\v 23 Avim, Parah, Ofrah,
\v 24 Kefar-Ha’amoni, Ofni, and Geva—a total of twelve cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\v 25 \add Also\add* Gibeon, Ramah, Be’erot,
\v 26 Mitspah, Kefirah, Motsah,
\v 27 Rekem, Yirpe’el, Tar’alah,
\v 28 Tsela, Haelef, Yevus (now called Jerusalem, Giveah, and Kiryat—\add a total of\add* fourteen cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages. Those were Benjamin’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans.
\c 19
\s1 The allocation to Simeon’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 The second draw went to Simeon’s descendants and the land for their clans was in the middle of Judah’s territory.
\v 2 Simeon’s inheritance included: Beersheba,\f + \fr 19:2 \ft TC: Ancient manuscripts differ, with some adding Sheba (possibly a mistaken reduplication) or Shema here (but that would make the total number wrong that’s given in v6).\f* Moladah,
\v 3 Hatsar-Shual, Balah, Etsem,
\v 4 Eltolad, Betul, Hormah,
\v 5 Tsiklag, Beyt-Hamarkavot. Hatsar-Susah,
\v 6 Beyt-Leva’ot, and Sharuhen—thirteen cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\p
\v 7 \add Also\add* Ayin, Rimon, Eter, and Asha—four cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages,
\v 8 and all the \add further\add* villages around those cities as far as Ba’alat-Be’er (\add also called\add* Ramah of the Negev). That was Simeon’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans.
\v 9 That \add took away\add* some of Judah’s inheritance and gave it to Simeon’s descendants because Judah’s \add initial\add* area was more than they needed.
\s1 The allocation to Zebulun’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 10 The third draw went to Zebulun’s descendants according to their clans. The \add southern\add* border for their inheritance started at Sarid
\v 11 and went \add west\add* to Maralah and Dabeshet then to the riverbed facing Yakeneam.
\v 12 \add Also\add* from Sarid it went toward the east beside the border with Kislot-Tavor and then on to Davrat and then to Yafia.
\v 13 From there it extended east to Gath-Hahefer and Et-Katsin and \add north\add* to Rimon before turning to Ne’ah.
\v 14 Then it passed north of Hanaton ending at Yiftah-El valley.
\v 15 \add It included the cities of\add* Katat, Nahalal, Shimron, Yid’alah, and Bethlehem—\add a total of\add* twelve cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\v 16 That was Zebulun’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans, \add including\add* those cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\s1 The allocation to Issachar’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 17 The fourth draw went to Issachar’s descendants according to their clans.
\v 18 Their land \add included the cities of\add* Jezreel, Kesulot, Shunem,
\v 19 Hafarayim, Shi’on, Anaharat,
\v 20 Rabbit, Kishyon, Evets,
\v 21 Remet, Eyn-Ganim, Eyn-Hadah, and Beyt-Patsets.
\v 22 The border \add of their allotment\add* met with Tavor, Shahatsimah, and Beyt-Shemesh, coming out \add in the east\add* at the Jordan—\add a total of\add* sixteen cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\v 23 That was Issachar’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans, \add including\add* those cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\s1 The allocation to Asher’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 24 The fifth draw went to Asher’s descendants according to their clans.
\v 25 Their land \add included the cities of\add* Helkat, Hali, Beten, Akshaf,
\v 26 Alamelek, Am’ad, and Mish’al. Their \add western\add* border started at \add Mt.\add* Carmel and Shihor-Livnat
\v 27 and turned back to the east to Beyt-Dagon and the border with Zebulun and the Yiftah-El valley to the north. Then it went to Beyt-Emek and Neiel and north to Kavul,
\v 28 Avdon, Rehov, Hamon and Kanah, as far as Greater Sidon.
\v 29 The border then turned back to Ramah and to the fortified city of Tyre and turns west to Hosah coming out at the Mediterranean near Akziv.
\v 30 \add It also included\add* Umah, Afek, and Rehov—\add a total of\add* twenty-two cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\v 31 That was Asher’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans, \add including\add* those cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\s1 The allocation to Naphtali’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 32 The sixth draw went to Naphtali’s descendants according to their clans.
\v 33 Their border started in Helef (where there was an oak tree at Tsa’ananim) then Adami-Nekev and Yavne’el and on to Lakum before exiting at the Jordan.
\v 34 Then it turned west to Aznot-Tavor and from there to Hukok, meeting \add the border\add* with Zebulun on the south, \add the border\add* with Asher on the west, and \add the border\add* with Judah on the east.
\v 35 The fortified cities were Tsidim, Tser, Hamat, Rakat, Kineret,
\v 36 Admah, Ramah, Hatsor,
\v 37 Kedesh, Edre’i, Eyn-Hatsor,
\v 38 Yir’on, Migdal-El, Horem, Beyt-Anat, and Beyt-Shemesh—\add a total of\add* nineteen cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\v 39 That was Naphtali’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans, \add including\add* those cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\s1 The allocation to Dan’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 40 The \add final\add* seventh draw went to Dan’s descendants according to their clans.
\v 41 Their land \add included the cities of\add* Tsor’ah, Eshtaol, Ir-Shemesh,
\v 42 Sha’alvim, Ayalon, and Yitlah,
\v 43 Eylon, Timnah, Ekron,
\v 44 Elteke, Gibton, Ba’alat,
\v 45 Yehud, Beney-Berak, Gath-Rimon,
\v 46 Mey-Hayarkon, Rakon, and the area opposite Joppa.
\v 47 Dan’s border went out from them.\f + \fr 19:47 \ft Translations vary widely here because we’re not sure what is meant by the literal rendering that we’ve kept here.\f* Then Dan’s descendants battled against Leshem and captured it and slaughtered their inhabitants. So they took it over and settled in Leshem but they renamed it to ‘Dan’ after their ancestor.
\v 48 That was Dan’s descendants’ inheritance according to their clans, \add including\add* those cities as well as their \add surrounding\add* villages.
\s1 Joshua’s allocation
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 49 After the allocation of the blocks of land to the various tribes, the Israelites gave Joshua Nun’s son) his \add own\add* inheritance among them.
\p
\v 50 Following Yahweh’s instructions to give Joshua whatever city he asked for, he was given Timnat-Serah in the Ephraim hill country and he built \add up\add* that city and lived in it.
\p
\v 51 Those were the allotments that Eleazar the priest and Joshua (Nun’s son) gave to the leaders of the Israelite tribes as their inheritances. It was done by throwing dice there in Shiloh in front of Yahweh at the opening of the \add sacred\add* tent, and so the land division was completed.
\c 20
\s1 The refuge cities
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Joshua,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites to select some cities that people can flee to in order to be safe, just as I instructed you through Moses,
\v 3 so that if someone accidentally kills someone else, they can escape there and be safe from the dead person’s family.
\v 4 When that person flees to one of these cities, \add they must\add* stand at the city gate and tell the elders there what happened. Then they should bring the person into the city and give them a place there to live in.
\v 5 Now if a relative of the deceased comes to avenge the death, the city elders won’t hand over the killer because the death was accidental and didn’t happen because of hate or premeditation.
\v 6 So then that person must stay in the city until they’re brought before the panel of judges. \add If they decide that the death wasn’t deliberate,\add* the person must remain in that city until the death of the high priest, then they can \add freely\add* return to their own city and own home.”
\p
\v 7 So the Israelites chose Kedesh (in the Galilee region in the Naphtali hill country) and Shechem (in the Ephraim hill country) and Kiryat-Arba (now called Hebron, in the Judah hill country).
\v 8 On the other side of the Jordan \add river\add*, they chose Betser (in the wilderness on the plateau from the Reuben tribe), Ramot (in the Gilead from the Gad tribe), and Golan (in the Bashan from the Manasseh tribe).
\v 9 Those were the appointed cities for both Israelites and foreigners living among them, so that anyone who accidentally killed someone could flee there and be safe from being avenged by the deceased’s relatives until they could receive a proper trial.
\c 21
\s1 The allocation to Levi’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then the leaders of the Levites went to Eleazar the priest and to Joshua Nun’s son) and to the Israelite elders
\v 2 at Shiloh (in the Canaan region) and told them, “Yahweh said through Moses that we should be given cities to live in, along with their surrounding fields for our livestock.”
\v 3 So the Israelite \add leaders\add* gave these cities and the fields around them to the Levites for their inheritance as Yahweh had instructed:
\p
\v 4 The first draw went to those of the Kohat clans \add who were\add* descendants of Aaron the priest (from the Levites). They were allocated thirteen cities in the regions occupied by Judah, Simeon, and Benjamin.
\v 5 The remaining Kohat clans were \add allocated\add* ten cities in the regions occupied by Ephraim, Dan, and the Manasseh half-tribe.
\p
\v 6 The Gereshon clans were \add allocated\add* thirteen cities in the regions occupied by Issachar, Asher, Naphtali, and by the Manasseh half-tribe in the Bashan \add region\add*.
\p
\v 7 The Merari clans were \add allocated\add* twelve cities in the regions occupied by Reuben, Gad, and Zebulun.
\p
\v 8 So the Israelite leaders gave those cities and their surrounding fields to the Levites, just as Yahweh had instructed through Moses.
\p
\v 9 These are the names of the cities allocated by the Israelite leaders (from the regions occupied by Judah and Simeon)
\v 10 that were given to the Levite Kohat clans since they were selected first in the draw:
\v 11 Kiryat-Arba and the surrounding fields (now called Hebron—Arba was the father of Anak) in Judah’s hill country
\v 12 (although the surrounding countryside and villages had already been given to Yefuneh’s son Caleb.
\v 13 So Hebron was given to the descendants of Aaron the priest and assigned as a refuge city (for anyone who \add accidentally\add* kills someone) along with the surrounding fields, along with Livnah,
\v 14 Yatir, Eshtemoa,
\v 15 Holon, Devir,
\v 16 Ayin, Yutah, and Beyt-Shemesh, all with their surrounding fields—\add a total of\add* nine cities from within Judah and Simeon’s \add territories\add*.
\v 17 From Benjamin’s \add territories\add*, \add the Israelite leaders\add* assigned Gibeon, Geva,
\v 18 Anatot, and Almon—four cities and their surrounding fields.
\v 19 \add Thus\add* the descendants of Aaron the priests \add received\add* thirteen cities with their surrounding fields.
\p
\v 20 The \add other\add* Levite Kohat clans were allocated cities from within Ephraim’s territory:
\v 21 Shechem (which was assigned as a refuge city for anyone who \add accidentally\add* kills someone), Gezer,
\v 22 Kivtsayim, and Beyt-Horon—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 23 From Dan’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Elteke, Gibton,
\v 24 Ayalon, and Gath-Rimon—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 25 From the Manasseh half-tribe’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Ta’anak and Gath-Rimon—two cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 26 \add Thus\add* the \add other\add* Levite Kohat clans \add received\add* ten cities with their surrounding fields.
\p
\v 27 The Levite Gereshon clans were allocated cities from within the Manasseh half-tribe’s territory: Golan in the Bashan (which was assigned as a refuge city for anyone who \add accidentally\add* kills someone) and Be’eshterah—two cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 28 From Issachar’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Kishyon, Davrat,
\v 29 Yarmut, and Eyn-Ganim—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 30 From Asher’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Mish’al, Avdon,
\v 31 Helkat, and Rehov—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 32 From Naphtali’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Kedesh in the Galilee (which was assigned as a refuge city for anyone who \add accidentally\add* kills someone), Hamot-Dor, and Kartan—three cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 33 \add Thus\add* the Levite Gereshon clan \add received\add* thirteen cities with their surrounding fields.
\p
\v 34 The remaining Levite Merari clans were allocated cities from within Zebulun’s territory: Yakeneam, Kartah,
\v 35 Dimnah, and Nahalal—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 36 From Reuben’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Betser, Yahats,
\v 37 Kedemot, and Mofa’at—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 38 From Gad’s \add territory\add*, they were allocated Ramot in the Gilead (which was assigned as a refuge city for anyone who \add accidentally\add* kills someone), Mahanayim,
\v 39 Heshbon, and Yazer—four cities with their surrounding fields.
\v 40 \add Thus\add* the Levite Merari clan \add received\add* twelve cities in the draw, along with their surrounding fields.
\p
\v 41 \add So\add* altogether the Levites received forty-eight cities and their surrounding fields from with the territories of the other tribes—
\v 42 each of those cities had fields around them \add for their livestock to graze on\add*.
\s1 The Israelites move into the regions
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 43 Thus Yahweh gave the Israelites all the land that he’d promised their ancestors would be given and \add so the people\add* moved into those regions and lived there.
\v 44 Yahweh gave them peace as he’d promised their ancestors—none of their enemies could stand against them because Yahweh helped them defeat any enemies.
\v 45 Yahweh kept every promise that he’d made to the Israelites—it all happened just like he’d said.
\c 22
\s1 The eastern tribes return home
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then Joshua summoned the Reuben and Gad tribes, and the Manasseh half-tribe
\v 2 and told them, “You yourselves have obeyed everything that Yahweh’s servant Moses instructed you to do. In addition, you’ve all followed everything that I told you.
\v 3 You’ve all supported your fellow tribes all this time and fulfilled what your god Yahweh requested of you all,
\v 4 and now he has enabled these other tribes to rest \add from battle\add* just like he told them he would. So now, you all may go back to your homes that Yahweh’s servant Moses gave you on the other side of the river.
\v 5 Only do take care to \add continue to\add* obey the instructions and the law that Yahweh’s servant Moses gave you: to love your god Yahweh, to follow him and his instructions, to stay close to him, and to serve him with all your hearts and your \add inner\add* selves.”
\v 6 Then Joshua blessed them and sent them on their way to return to their homes.
\v 7 (Moses had given the Bashan to the half of the Manasseh half-tribe, and Joshua had given \add land\add* on the western side of the Jordan to the other half.) \add When\add* he’d blessed them
\v 8 he’d said, “Go back to your homes with many riches: plenty of livestock, and with silver, gold, bronze, and iron, and with lots of clothing. Share the plunder from your enemies with those who remained behind.”
\v 9 So the warriors from the Reuben and Gad \add tribes\add*, and the Manasseh half-tribe left the others there at Shiloh in the Canaan region, and went back to their own land in Gilead \add across the river\add* which they had taken for themselves with the agreement of Yahweh given through Moses.
\s1 The altar by the Jordan
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 10 The warriors from the Reuben and Gad \add tribes\add*, and the Manasseh half-tribe arrived at the Jordan \add river\add* (\add still\add* on the Canaan side) and they built a very large and noticeable altar there \add before crossing over to go home\add*.
\v 11 \add When\add* the \add other\add* Israelites heard \add about it\add*, they said, “Look, the men from the Reuben and Gad \add tribes\add*, and the Manasseh half-tribe have built the altar at the entrance to this Canaan region down at the Jordan on our side.”
\p
\v 12 Hearing that, those from the western side of the Jordan assembled at Shiloh to go to battle with the two and a half tribes on the eastern side.
\v 13 \add Meanwhile\add*, they sent the priest Pinhas (Eleazar’s son) \add across\add* into the Gilead region \add to talk\add* with \add the leaders from\add* the Reuben and Gad tribes, and the Manasseh half-tribe.
\v 14 Pinhas took ten leaders with him—important leaders from each of the Israelite tribes \add from the western side\add*.
\v 15 They \add crossed over\add* to the Gilead region to talk to \add the leaders from\add* the Reuben and Gad tribes, and the Manasseh half-tribe. They said,
\v 16 “Yahweh’s people are asking, ‘What’s this unfaithfulness that you all have done unfaithfully against Israel’s god—turning away from Yahweh when you built an altar for yourselves today in rebellion against Yahweh?
\v 17 Wasn’t our disobedience at Pe’or bad enough for you? We haven’t gotten over it to this day. Plus there was a deadly disease among Yahweh’s people.
\v 18 But all of you—are you turning away from Yahweh today? If you all rebel against Yahweh today, tomorrow he’ll be furious at all of us.
\v 19 But if it’s because you all think this land here is defiled, then cross back over to Yahweh’s own land where he lives in his sacred tent. Either way don’t rebel against Yahweh by building \add this other\add* altar for yourselves that’s distinct from our god Yahweh’s altar.
\v 20 Didn’t Akan act unfaithfully taking something that was forbidden and all of us suffered as a result of it? It wasn’t just him alone that died because of that.’ ”
\p
\v 21 \add The leaders from\add* the Reuben and Gad tribes, and the Manasseh half-tribe answered the other leaders,
\v 22 “Yahweh is the god of gods. Yahweh is the god of gods. He knows, and as for the Israelite people, let them know \add also\add*—if it was in rebellion or in unfaithfulness against Yahweh, don’t bother trying to save us today.
\v 23 If we’d aimed to build an altar for ourselves to turn \add away\add* from following Yahweh, or if it was to offer burnt offerings or grain offerings on it, or if to make the sacrifices of peace offerings on it, Yahweh himself will handle it.
\v 24 But no, we did it because we foresaw a potential problem: what if in the future, your descendants ask our descendants \add over here on this side of the river\add*, ‘What do you lot have to do with Israel’s god Yahweh?
\v 25 Yahweh has given this Jordan river as a border between us, and you descendants of Reuben and Gad. You all don’t have any part in Yahweh.’ Then your descendants might make our descendants stop worshipping Yahweh.
\v 26 So we decided to build the altar—not to be used for burnt offerings or for sacrifices,
\v 27 but as a symbol to remind our future descendants and yours, that we all truly worship Yahweh with our burnt offerings, our sacrifices, and our peace offerings, so your descendants can’t tell our future descendants that we have no business worshipping Yahweh.
\v 28 If they do say anything like that, then our descendants will be able to reply, ‘See that copy of Yahweh’s altar which isn’t used for burnt offerings or sacrifices, our ancestors built that as a reminder for both you all and us.’
\v 29 May any idea of rebelling against Yahweh stay far from us. Same with any idea of building any altar apart from our god Yahweh’s \add genuine\add* altar for burnt offerings, or grain offerings, or other sacrifices.”
\p
\v 30 So Pinhas the priest and the other community and clan leaders with him, listened to what the descendants of Reuben, Gad, and Manasseh told them, and it sounded sensible to them,
\v 31 so Pinhas (\add the late\add* Eleazar’s son) told them, “Today we realise that Yahweh is among us and that you all haven’t acted unfaithfully against him, so therefore you’ve prevented Yahweh from needing to punish us.”
\p
\v 32 Then Pinhas and the other leaders left Gilead and returned back \add across the Jordan\add* to the Canaan region where most of the Israelites resided, taking back a \add good\add* report.
\v 33 The people were pleased with that news, and they thanked God and stopped talking about going to war with Reuben and Gad \add across the river\add* and destroying them.
\p
\v 34 The descendants of Reuben and Gad named the altar \add ‘Reminder’\add* because it was a reminder between them that Yahweh is God.
\c 23
\s1 Joshua’s final speech
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 A long time passed and Yahweh had given Israel peace without attacks from their enemies all around, and Joshua was \add now\add* in his old age,
\v 2 so he summoned all the Israelite elders and leaders, along with the judges and officials. He told them, “\add Now\add* I’m in my old age,
\v 3 but you all have seen what Yahweh did to all those kingdoms ahead of you, because he’s the one who fights for you all.
\v 4 I’ve allocated the remaining kingdoms that you all will defeat as an inheritance for your tribes, alongside those that I conquered from the Jordan to the Mediterranean in the west
\v 5 Your god Yahweh will push them out ahead of you all and capture their lands for you to possess, just as he told you he would.
\v 6 Take great care to obey and do everything that Moses wrote down—don’t deviate from it in any way.
\v 7 Don’t enter those remaining kingdoms, and don’t mention the name of their gods or make an oath using their names and don’t worship or bow down to them.
\v 8 Instead you all should cling to your god Yahweh, just as you’ve done until now.
\v 9 \add Yes,\add* Yahweh has driven away many great kingdoms ahead of you all, and until this day, none of them have stood against you.
\v 10 Just one of you can make a thousand men flee, because Yahweh is your god—he’s the one who fights for you just like he said he would.
\v 11 Make a great effort to guard yourselves that you all \add continue\add* to love your god Yahweh.
\v 12 \add Yes,\add* if you all ever turn away \add from him\add* and instead cling to those remaining kingdoms or remaining people, and if you intermarry with them or associate with them,
\v 13 you all will certainly \add soon\add* find out that your god Yahweh will not continue to drive out these nations ahead of you. Then they will \add end up\add* trapping and snaring you all, and be like a whip on your sides and like thorns in your eyes, until you all perish from on this good land that Yahweh your god has given you.
\p
\v 14 Listen, today I’m about to go the way that everyone has to go and you all know with all your heart and with all your soul that not one thing has failed to come true from all the good things that your god Yahweh said about you. Everything has happened as said—down to the very last word.
\v 15 And just like all those good things came true, so \add too\add* Yahweh will send all the punishments he promised you all \add if you disobey him\add*, even if it means that he destroys you all from this good land that he’s given you.
\v 16 If you ignore the agreement with your god Yahweh and \add disobey\add* his commands and go and serve other gods and bow down to them, then his anger will flare up against you all and you’ll quickly be wiped out from the good land that he has given you.”
\c 24
\s1 The promise again of Israel’s descendants
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 1 Then Joshua assembled all the tribes of Israel at Shechem. He summoned their elders, leaders, judges, and officers, and they presented themselves before God,
\v 2 and Joshua told all the people, “Yahweh, the god of Israel says: Your ancestors \add including\add* Terah the father of Abraham and Nahor, lived beyond the \add Euphrates\add* river from long ago, but they worshipped other gods.
\v 3 Then I took your ancestor Abraham from beyond the river and brought him across all of Canaan, and I multiplied his descendants, including giving him Isaac.
\v 4 Then I gave Jacob and Esau to Isaac, and I gave Esau the Se’ir hill country but Jacob and his sons went down to Egypt.
\v 5 I sent Moses and Aaron and I damaged Egypt internally, and then I brought you all out \add again\add*.
\v 6 I brought your ancestors out of Egypt, and you all came to the \add edge\add* of the Red sea. The Egyptians chased after them with chariots and horsemen
\v 7 and the people cried out to Yahweh, and he placed darkness between you and the Egyptians. \add After bringing you all through the sea,\add* he brought the sea over the Egyptian army and drowned them. Your \add own\add* eyes saw what I did to Egypt. Then you all lived a long time in the wilderness.
\p
\v 8 “\add After that,\add* I brought you to the region of the Amorites who lived on the eastern side of the Jordan, and they battled against you all, but I gave you victory over them and I destroyed them from ahead of you and you all took over their land.
\v 9 Then (Tsipor’s son) King Balak of Moab prepared his warriors and fought against you all and he summoned (Beor’s son) Balaam to curse you.
\v 10 But I wasn’t willing to listen to Balaam, so instead, he blessed you all repeatedly and I rescued you from King Balak.
\v 11 Then you crossed over the Jordan \add river\add* and arrived at Jericho. The men of Jericho fought against you all, \add as did\add* the Amorites, the Perizim, the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Girgashim, the Hivim, and the Jebusites but I helped you all to defeat them.
\v 12 I sent hornets\f + \fr 24:12 \ft The Hebrew word ‘צִרְעָה’ (‘hornet’, but pluralised here in the OET-RV) may have some non-literal meaning here, e.g., ‘panic’, or it may be a general reference to any manifestation of God’s anger.\f* ahead of you all and they drove out the two Amorite kings—it wasn’t your swords or bows that did it.
\v 13 So I gave you all a land which you didn’t earn yourselves and cities to live in that you all didn’t build yourselves. You all are benefitting from vineyards and olive groves that you didn’t plant.”
\p
\v 14 \add The Joshua\add* \add continued,\add* “Now you all must respect Yahweh and faithfully serve him with your complete beings. And you all must remove the idols that your ancestors served beyond the Euphrates and in Egypt, and \add only\add* serve Yahweh.
\v 15 But if you all dislike serving Yahweh, then choose for yourselves today who you will serve: perhaps the gods your fathers \add once\add* served beyond the Euphrates, or the gods of the Amorite \add people\add* in whose land you are \add now\add* living. But as for me and my household, we will serve Yahweh.”
\p
\v 16 “We’d never even consider leaving Yahweh to serve other gods,” the people responded,
\v 17 “because Yahweh is our god. He is the one who brought us and our ancestors out from slavery in Egypt, and who did those great miracles right in front of our eyes. Also, he looked after us on our entire journey and among all the people groups that we passed by.
\v 18 Yahweh drove all the peoples out ahead of us, even the Amorites who lived in that region. \add So\add* we too we will serve Yahweh because he is our god.”
\p
\v 19 “You all won’t be able to serve Yahweh,” warned Joshua, “because he’s a god who cannot mix with sin. He’s a jealous God—he won’t forgive your rebellion and disobedience.
\v 20 If you abandon Yahweh and serve the gods of other nations, then he’ll turn and do harm to you all and destroy you, even if he’s previously been good to you all.”
\p
\v 21 But the people answered Joshua, “No, we will worship Yahweh.”
\v 22 Then Joshua cautioned the people, “You all are witnesses against yourselves, that you yourselves have chosen to serve Yahweh.”
\p “\add We are\add* witnesses,” they confirmed.
\p
\v 23 “Then \add you all go right\add* now and remove the foreign gods that you already have, and turn your hearts towards Yahweh, the god of Israel,” \add said Joshua\add*.
\p
\v 24 “We will serve our god Yahweh, and we will listen to his voice,” the people replied.
\p
\v 25 So Joshua made a formal agreement with the people in Shechem on that day, establishing rules and laws for them.
\v 26 He wrote them in the book of God’s law. Then he took a large stone and set it up there beneath the oak tree that was by Yahweh’s sacred tent.
\v 27 “Look at this stone,” Joshua said to all the people. “It will be for a witness against us, because it has heard everything that Yahweh has instructed us, and it will be for a witness against you all, in case you act deceptively against your god.”
\v 28 Then Joshua sent \add all\add* the people off to go to their allotted regions.
\s1 The deaths of Joshua and Eleazar
\s4 Promised land
\p
\v 29 After all that, Nun’s son and Yahweh’s servant Joshua died at the age of 110.
\v 30 They buried him in his allotted property at Timnat-Serah which is in the Ephraim hill country north of Mt. Ga’ash.
\p
\v 31 The Israelites had served Yahweh all through Joshua’s lifetime and throughout the lifetimes of the elders who lived longer than Joshua and who had experienced everything that Yahweh had done for Israel.
\p
\v 32 They buried Joseph’s bones (that they had carried out from Egypt in Shechem—in the portion of the field that Jacob had bought from the sons of Hamor (Shechem’s father) for a hundred coins, and that land became the inheritance of Joseph’s descendants.
\p
\v 33 Aaron’s son Eleazar \add also\add* died and was buried at Giveah—\add the city\add* that belonged to his son Pinhas in the Ephraim hill country.
